│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multi_restart_greedy_best_of_n                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ A single greedy run is highly dependent on its random choices (both the starting          │
│                          │ transaction and the random sampling of candidate transactions). Running many randomized   │
│                          │ greedy restarts and keeping the schedule with the lowest makespan is a classic and very   │
│                          │ effective way to lower the final makespan without changing the per-run cost. Each restart │
│                          │ explores a different region of the ordering space; taking the min over restarts           │
│                          │ monotonically improves (or at least never worsens) the expected result. Additionally,     │
│                          │ lowering the sample_rate slightly (e.g., 0.8) injects occasional random "exploration"     │
│                          │ picks, which helps escape locally greedy traps, while num_samples=10 keeps the greedy     │
│                          │ evaluation strong. We run e.g. 100 restarts within the greedy and return the best found.  │
