│ patch_description        │ The current solution is a full-lookahead greedy with random restarts. Greedy alone gets   │
│                          │ stuck in local optima. I propose two improvements:                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Keep the multistart greedy (it's effective), but with a time budget per greedy run.    │
│                          │ 2. After each greedy solution, apply a local search (insertion + adjacent swap moves):    │
│                          │ repeatedly try moving a transaction to a different position in the sequence (an effective │
│                          │ neighborhood for sequencing problems, like insertion neighborhoods in TSP), and accept    │
│                          │ any move that reduces makespan. This directly optimizes the actual objective (makespan    │
│                          │ from `get_opt_seq_cost`) rather than heuristics, and can escape greedy's local optima     │
│                          │ since a bad greedy choice can be relocated later.                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The local search runs within a global time budget and keeps the best overall solution.    │
│                          │ Insertion moves are O(n²) candidates per pass, and since makespan is monotone in prefix   │
│                          │ conflicts, moving conflicting transactions earlier/later often yields real gains. This    │
│                          │ should push makespan below the current 299.                                               │
