│ patch_name               │ simulated_annealing_local_search                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current local search is a pure hill climber with insertion moves evaluated by full    │
│                          │ re-computation of the sequence cost, which gets stuck in local minima and spends budget   │
│                          │ on O(n^2) full-cost evaluations per pass. I propose replacing it with a time-budgeted     │
│                          │ simulated annealing over swap and insertion moves. SA accepts occasional worsening moves  │
│                          │ with probability exp(-delta/T), allowing escape from local minima, which for permutation  │
│                          │ scheduling problems (this is essentially a single-machine sequencing problem with         │
│                          │ conflict-dependent setup costs) is known to substantially outperform greedy descent. We   │
│                          │ also seed SA from multiple top beam candidates rather than just the best, increasing      │
│                          │ diversity of the search basins explored. Full cost evaluation per move is kept (necessary │
│                          │ since makespan depends on the whole ordering), but the acceptance logic is cheap and the  │
│                          │ move selection is randomized so the budget is spread over promising neighborhoods rather  │
│                          │ than exhaustively scanning all O(n^2) insertions in order.                                │
