│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_beam_width_restarts                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The main compounding error in beam search happens early, where a wrong pruning eliminates │
│                          │ large swaths of the solution space. I make two targeted improvements: (1) use a wide beam │
│                          │ during the first 25% of placements (where branching decisions compound most) and narrow   │
│                          │ it later — this concentrates the search budget where pruning error matters, as            │
│                          │ recommended; (2) scale the number of restarts with a time check rather than a hard cap so │
│                          │ the full time budget is used across restarts (more diverse starting points), and track    │
│                          │ time more tightly so local search still gets its share. Additionally, in local search I   │
│                          │ add a randomized perturbation restart (accepting slightly worse solutions) when           │
│                          │ insertion/swap moves stagnate, which helps escape local minima.                           │
