│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ beam_search_adaptive_width                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the returned single-sample greedy with a beam search over transaction orderings.  │
│                          │ Each beam state holds a partial sequence and its makespan cost; at each step we expand    │
│                          │ every state with all remaining transactions, evaluate via `get_opt_seq_cost`, and keep    │
│                          │ the best `beam_width` states. Beam search directly optimizes actual makespan (not proxies │
│                          │ like length/write-count), corrects greedy mistakes by keeping several candidates alive,   │
│                          │ and is far more sample-efficient than random restarts of a 1-sample greedy. We use a      │
│                          │ depth-adaptive width (wide early, e.g. 6–12, where pruning error compounds most; narrow   │
│                          │ late, e.g. 3) and run a few restarts with different initial transactions for diversity,   │
│                          │ keeping the best overall schedule.                                                        │
