                        Patch Metadata - Gen 28/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ enhanced_local_search_moves                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The current local search only uses single-insertion and pairwise-swap neighborhoods,      │
│                          │ which can get stuck in local optima that segment-based moves (Or-opt: move blocks of 2-3  │
│                          │ consecutive transactions; 2-opt: reverse a segment) can escape. Transaction makespan is   │
│                          │ highly sensitive to the *relative order* of conflicting transactions, and reversing a     │
│                          │ segment changes many pairwise orders at once — a powerful move for this cost landscape.   │
│                          │ Additionally, the current implementation is best-improvement per pass, which wastes time  │
│                          │ re-evaluating; switching insertion/swap to first-improvement with a wrap-around restart   │
│                          │ of the scan speeds up each pass so more perturbation restarts fit in the time budget. I   │
│                          │ add these moves to `local_search` while keeping the existing perturbation restart loop    │
│                          │ unchanged, so the overall hybrid structure (beam search + GRASP + ILS) and its proven     │
│                          │ score are preserved, only the descent neighborhood is enriched.                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Step-by-step reasoning:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Insertion/swap moves explore a small neighborhood; Or-opt (moving short contiguous     │
│                          │ blocks) and 2-opt (segment reversal) are standard, complementary moves for sequencing     │
│                          │ problems and escape different local optima.                                               │
│                          │ 2. First-improvement (accept immediately on gain, continue scanning from the next index)  │
│                          │ reduces cost-function evaluations per pass, giving more perturbation restarts within the  │
│                          │ same 20s budget.                                                                          │
│                          │ 3. All moves preserve permutation validity (each txn appears exactly once), so schedule   │
│                          │ validity (validity: 1.00) is maintained.                                                  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
