│                          │ variant strong, and its beam keeps only cost-top children so all beam slots converge to   │
│                          │ near-identical prefixes. I restore the richer move set (insertion, swap, or-opt blocks of │
│                          │ size 2-3, 2-opt reversals) with first-improvement early exits so each pass spends its     │
│                          │ evaluations where gains are found, and add diversity-preserving beam pruning: keep the    │
│                          │ top beam_width-2 by cost plus 2 children whose last-appended txn differs from the         │
│                          │ cost-best entries. This prevents the beam from wasting all slots on one prefix while      │
│                          │ keeping greedy-style diversity, which should lower makespan further below 274. I also add │
│                          │ a short candidate-sequence dedup cache on get_opt_seq_cost to avoid recomputing identical │
│                          │ sequences across passes.                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Step-by-step reasoning:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Insertion/swap alone get stuck in shallow local optima for ordering problems; block    │
│                          │ moves (or-opt) and segment reversals (2-opt) escape wider basins, so restoring them       │
│                          │ yields lower local minima.                                                                │
│                          │ 2. First-improvement with break exits finds improving moves faster, allowing more         │
│                          │ perturbation restarts within the fixed 20s budget — more effective search iterations      │
│                          │ means better final schedules.                                                             │
│                          │ 3. Diverse beam pruning maintains exploration of multiple txn-ordering frontiers at zero  │
│                          │ extra cost, feeding the local search with genuinely different starting candidates (the    │
│                          │ same diversity principle that made greedy constructions effective).                       │
│                          │ 4. A memo dict avoids recomputing costs of sequences already evaluated (perturbations and │
│                          │ moves often revisit states), freeing time for additional moves.                           │
