                        Patch Metadata - Gen 35/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ beam_cost_reuse_and_ils_refactor                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ Reasoning step-by-step:                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Diagnose the bottleneck.** In the beam loop, every child's cost is computed once via │
│                          │ `get_opt_seq_cost(new_seq)` and stored in the candidate tuple — yet at the end of each    │
│                          │ restart the code calls `get_opt_seq_cost(seq)` *again* on every beam entry. This is pure  │
│                          │ wasted evaluation. Reusing the already-stored cost `c` frees budget for wider beams /     │
│                          │ more restarts.                                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Phase 2 is the biggest lever.** The top prior program (combined score 3623.19,       │
│                          │ makespan 275) used *iterated local search* (ILS) — first-improvement insertion, swap,     │
│                          │ or-opt block moves, and segment-reversal — with perturbation restarts, and it beat every  │
│                          │ simulated-annealing variant tried (3521, 3546, 3584). SA wastes many accepted uphill      │
│                          │ moves; ILS spends nearly all evaluations on improving or perturb-then-improve moves,      │
│                          │ which empirically yields better makespans here.                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Diversity matters.** The winning ILS program also deduplicated candidates by         │
│                          │ sequence so the local search budget isn't spent re-optimizing identical seeds.            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ So I apply: (a) reuse stored beam costs, (b) replace the SA phase with the proven ILS     │
│                          │ (first-improvement local search + shuffled-segment perturbation restarts), (c) dedupe     │
│                          │ candidates. This should recover the best-known performance while keeping the beam         │
│                          │ construction phase unchanged.                                                             │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0218                                                                                   │
