│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ budgeted_ils_insertion_swap                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The previous program spent its whole budget on beam-search restarts and greedy            │
│                          │ constructions but never refined the best solutions found. The key insight is that beam    │
│                          │ search gets you close quickly, while iterated local search (ILS) with insertion moves —   │
│                          │ relocating a transaction to every other position — exploits the structure of conflict     │
│                          │ delays far better than more randomized restarts. This design: (1) spends ~30% of the      │
│                          │ budget on greedy+beam constructions for diverse starting points, (2) spends the remaining │
│                          │ ~70% on ILS where each iteration applies a best-improvement insertion sweep over the      │
│                          │ current solution, followed by random perturbation (random 3-relocation) when stuck, with  │
│                          │ acceptance of only improving solutions and restarts from the second-best candidate if     │
│                          │ stagnation persists. Full-sequence cost evaluation keeps correctness identical to the     │
│                          │ original interface.                                                                       │
